Hmm, my thoughts in terms like "blocking" visibility wasn't meant
literally. But now, where you say it, I think I was too fast with my "yes
and amen" to Seraphim. Seraphim suggests that both 'normal' (visible)
light and infrared rays are physically the same, just on a different
spectrum. While this is basically true, there's still a big difference:
What we usually see is the reflection of light (except real light
sources), means that our eyes needs an external light source while an
infrared view is always a view of the source itself! Thus *blocking* light
to gain invisibility doesn't work: It would also block the background,
revealing a scheme of the object.

